"""Long-form narrative prompt for testing large paragraphs and readability."""

CAPTAIN_LOG = """# Captain's Log, Stardate 47892.4

The nebula we entered three days ago has proven more labyrinthine than any star chart suggested. **Sensors are unreliable** here; every reading shifts like sand. I have ordered the helm to maintain a constant bearing by dead reckoning, and to trust the astrometric arrays only when they agree with at least two independent calculations.

*First Officer Spence has raised the question of turning back.* I understand her caution. The crew is tired. We have been at full alert for seventy-two hours, and the strange interference that blankets this region plays tricks on the mind—shadows in the corners of the eye, the sense that someone has just left the room. I have asked Dr. Voss to monitor everyone for fatigue and to report any hallucinations at once. So far she has found nothing clinical. Only the ordinary unease that comes from staring into the void too long.

We are not turning back. The signal we are chasing—that faint, repeating pattern that might be a distress call or might be something else entirely—grew stronger this morning. Whatever is waiting for us in the heart of this cloud, we will meet it with clear eyes and a steady hand.

End log entry.
"""

SECOND_ENTRY = """# Supplemental

I have decided to record a second entry today because the events of the past six hours deserve a fuller account than the brief entry I made earlier. What we found at the coordinates suggested by the signal was not a ship. It was a **station**—old, derelict, its hull pitted and scored by micrometeorites and something that might have been weapons fire. The design does not match any known species in our database.

We are docked now. The boarding party has returned from their first sweep with preliminary reports: breathable atmosphere, minimal power, no signs of recent habitation. And yet the signal continues to emanate from somewhere deep in the structure. Tomorrow we will go back with a larger team and better equipment. I want to know who built this place, and why they called for help—or what is left of them that still does.

The crew is restless. I do not blame them. There is something about empty corridors and silent machinery that gets under the skin. I have doubled the watch and ordered that no one goes alone. We will be careful. We will be thorough. And we will find the source of that signal.
"""

THIRD_ENTRY = """# Supplemental (continued)

The second sweep yielded more questions than answers. We have mapped seven levels so far. The architecture is *wrong* in ways that are hard to describe—corridors that seem to curve back on themselves when you are not looking, bulkheads that hum at frequencies just below hearing. Lieutenant Chen swears she saw a door that was not there on the first pass. Dr. Voss has taken samples of the wall material; her preliminary report suggests it is partly organic, or was grown rather than forged. I have never heard of such a thing.

We have located the source of the signal. It originates from a sealed chamber on the lowest level we have reached. The door will not respond to our tools or our codes. There appears to be a kind of interface—perhaps a lock, perhaps something else—but our linguists and engineers are still arguing over whether the symbols represent language, mathematics, or something we do not yet have a name for. I have requested that we attempt no forced entry until we understand more. Whatever is behind that door has waited a long time. It can wait a little longer.
"""

FOURTH_ENTRY = """# Supplemental (continued)

This morning, one of the work crews reported a temperature drop in Section Gamma—a corridor we had already cleared and marked as stable. When the team returned to investigate, they found a new passageway. *It was not on any of our maps.* The walls there are warmer than the rest of the station, and the hum is louder. We have sent a small team to explore, with orders to maintain continuous contact and turn back at the first sign of structural instability.

In the meantime, Ensign Rivera has made progress on the sealed chamber. She believes the interface may be keyed to biological markers rather than logic—a lock that recognizes *who* you are, not what you know. If she is right, we may need to find whatever passed for the station's crew, or their remains. The thought is not a pleasant one. I have ordered a search for any compartment that might have served as living quarters or a medical bay. We need to understand who they were before we can hope to open that door.
"""
